WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.560
SHARON HARRIS: Hello, and
thank you for joining us.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.900
I know it's really
late in the day,

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.070
but we're so happy to see
all of your faces here.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.540
My name is Sharon Harris.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.600
I am the VP of
Alliances at Deloitte.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.090
And I oversee our relationship
with the Google Marketing

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.050
Platform.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.000
And I am thrilled
today to be joined

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.540
by my partner and my
technologist extraordinaire,

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.730
Mr. Matt Mager.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.150
MATT MAGER: Hi, I'm Matt Mager.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.790
I'm a Global Partner Solutions
Lead on the Google Marketing

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.070
Platform and the Ads products.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.940
SHARON HARRIS: So
today we're going

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.830
to share with you how the Google
Cloud Platform and the Google

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.770
Marketing Platform
are coming together.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:51.740
And this is really exciting,
particularly for us marketers,

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.472
because it's all about, how can
we leverage this technology?

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:56.930
And today we're
going to look at it

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.210
through the lens of a
marketer in the retail space.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.450
Retail is an industry
that's really near and dear

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.620
to my heart.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.990
And it's also an
industry that's really

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.590
experiencing a ton of
change, a lot of disruption,

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.350
and certainly it's under siege.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.640
Whether you're a brick
and mortar store,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.510
whether you're online,
a mom and pop shop,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.430
everyone is really
engaged in the battle

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:21.560
today when it comes to retail.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.450
Using retail as our
focus, we're really

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.600
going to showcase the power
of marketing analytics

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.525
on the Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:28.525 --> 00:01:29.900
And most importantly,
what we now

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:35.510
refer to as cloud for
marketing, so let's get started.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.790
I have this slide for
a couple of reasons.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.080
One, when you think
about it, at least 40%

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.360
of the businesses
that we know today

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.690
are going to die in
the next 10 years.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.040
It's a very ominous slide.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.780
But I think it's
really important

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.800
because it articulates in a
very clear way what's at stake.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.910
I do believe, however, it
does provide an insight

00:01:57.910 --> 00:01:59.800
into what we're going
to talk about today,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.690
in that there's still hope.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.150
While we've seen many
of our beloved retailers

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:08.110
fade away, whether it's
Brookstone, or Henry Brindle.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.400
Or we've seen stores close
like Payless, or Gymboree,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.160
or Victoria's Secret,
there's still hope.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.040
One of my favorite
retailers closed, Toys R

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:22.330
Us I was really sad to
see Jeffrey pack his bags,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.000
make that last exit
out of the store

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.630
because that was a fond
part of my childhood.

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:30.790
That was just an
iconic brand for me.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.040
Everyone loved Toys R Us.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.080
But this quote
really symbolizes,

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.920
this is where we are right now.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:38.805
And this is really
a critical juncture.

00:02:38.805 --> 00:02:40.180
If you're in the
audience, saying

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.670
you're working for a retailer
or you're a marketer,

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.130
this is really a
pivotal moment today.

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:50.270
Now I've spent over 20 years
in the marketing space--

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.280
a variety of different roles.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.750
And I can tell you firsthand.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:57.800
The job of a marketer is very
difficult. The average CMO,

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.250
their tenure is, if they're
lucky, 18 to 24 months,

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.240
if you're not showing results.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.920
And those challenges
are continuing to evolve

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.190
and there are new concerns,
but there are new opportunities

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.480
at every turn.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.040
Top of the list of challenges
is, how do you keep pace

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.930
with the technological change?

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.390
All of you are here
at Google Cloud Next

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.060
because you know what those
next technological changes are

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.110
looking like.

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.520
And that's really important.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.000
And more and more marketers are
starting to come to the table

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.160
and understand how to
leverage that change.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.700
But when you look at
those top five challenges,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.327
we've got other
things to think about.

00:03:36.327 --> 00:03:38.660
We've got to think about, how
do we get the right talent

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.560
in our organization?

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.920
How do we create
engaging digital content?

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.940
How do we have the right
skills in our business?

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.980
And one of my
favorites is, how do we

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.430
measure the effectiveness
of digital content?

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.120
As a marketer, I can't
manage what I can't measure

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.810
and that's really critical.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.620
And that's where, once again,
the technology can truly

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.510
help me.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:08.150
I was really sad to see
Sears file bankruptcy.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.340
Sears, the original superstore.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.100
The precursor, if you
will, to the Amazon.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.750
Sears, an iconic American brand.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.720
Everything you wanted,
from batteries, to dresses,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.140
to Kenmore appliances, to tires.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:24.980
Everything.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.110
MATT MAGER: Houses.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.680
SHARON HARRIS: Houses too.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.240
Everything under one roof.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:31.820
Garanimals, chubby kid clothes.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:36.980
Everything you could possibly
need under one umbrella.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.550
That was the destination for
the family on the weekends.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:42.500
If your family was
like mine, Sears,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:46.160
we got the tires rotated, we
got a new battery, picked up

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.550
some clothes, some
fabric even, everything

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.650
under that single store.

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:54.590
I was really sad to
see them say farewell

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.400
because it seemed to be the end
of a major era in our country.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.640
But I say this
because I do believe

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.440
that there is still hope.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.500
And today Matt and I are going
to share with you some things

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.320
that we believe will
help you to avoid some

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.990
of the pitfalls many of
these other retailers

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.970
have experienced.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.970
Now, it goes without saying.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.270
A key issue for a
marketer today is trust.

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:23.060
Consumer trust is paramount.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.560
We've all heard the horror
stories of data breaches.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.620
The demise of many
retailers, a lot of it's

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.997
around they didn't
leverage their data.

00:05:32.997 --> 00:05:34.580
And we all know,
everyone in this room

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.040
knows, we talk about
data constantly.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.380
Data is the number one
strategic priority of marketers,

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:43.250
and especially retailers.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:45.330
Data is that new currency.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:46.640
It's the new oil.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.920
It's the lifeblood
of the organization.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.370
And if you don't really
have a handle on it,

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:53.810
you're not going
to be successful

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.850
in this current landscape.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:59.700
Now, in advertising,
we often say,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:04.640
not curing cancer and not making
the world safe for mankind,

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.340
we're just making ads.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.700
Unlike physicians, marketers,
we don't take an oath.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.730
But today, I want
us to take an oath.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.040
As a marketer, the
oath I want you to take

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.130
is really critical and
it goes like this--

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:25.590
as a marketer, I vow to know
my customer, be my customer,

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.530
and empathize with my customer.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.990
Those are the critical things
you must do as a marketer.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:35.030
But of course, you might
be asking yourself,

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.350
where do I begin?

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.910
I've got lots of data.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.500
Lots of siloed data.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.100
I've got different
organizations,

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:42.440
different stakeholders.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.960
I've got the finance person
breathing down my neck

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.620
going, what are you spending
all that marketing money on?

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.470
You've got lots of challenges
that you're facing.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.130
So where do you begin?

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.030
The technology.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:02.190
MATT MAGER: And traditionally
for marketers using Google,

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:06.360
that technology has been the
Google Marketing Platform.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.520
That's been the
place where marketers

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.770
have gone to do the work
of knowing their customers

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:12.570
and empathizing
with them better.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.930
But they haven't always
been called that.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.720
So who here has used one
of these products before?

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:20.650
Lots of hands.

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:22.650
SHARON HARRIS: Raise them
high, raise them high.

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:26.910
MATT MAGER: Analytics,
DBM, now DV360, yes.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.370
Keep your hands up if you
have used more than one

00:07:29.370 --> 00:07:31.020
of these products.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.100
Great.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.310
So you know already,
and most of you

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.410
here have seen or
sit next to someone

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.560
who does, know that these
products have been loved

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.570
and used for years,
even though they

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.750
haven't been called the Google
Marketing Platform together.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.060
Marketing analytics
products can already

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.780
share touch points and behaviors
in a privacy-positive way,

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:55.800
allowing brands to drive the
relevance that makes ads useful

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.590
and build the
relationships that users

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.950
need to ensure that the
brands stay top of mind.

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:04.850
The unified Google
Marketing Platform

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:10.010
is comprised of four
products along media buying,

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:13.680
ad serving, and
creative management,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.110
and four analytics products on
tag management, site analytics,

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:22.870
data visualization, and
web page optimization.

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:26.520
This is the legacy Analytics
360 Suite and the DoubleClick

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:27.657
Platform.

00:08:27.657 --> 00:08:30.240
Together these products make up
the Google Marketing Platform,

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.570
which is a really great place
to start to know and empathize

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:34.440
with your customers.

00:08:37.505 --> 00:08:38.880
But the Google
Marketing Platform

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:41.159
is really only part
of the picture.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:44.370
I mean, we're not at GML.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.440
If we take the ads data and
the marketing data alone,

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.505
we can only do so much.

00:08:48.505 --> 00:08:50.130
And for those of you
who are marketers,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.800
you're probably doing as
much as you already can.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.020
What we need to do is we
need to use the data that's

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.680
available to do more interesting
things, more in-depth things,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.852
to know and to communicate
better with our customers.

00:09:03.852 --> 00:09:05.310
Think about the
business challenges

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.520
you could solve if you added
your point of sale data

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.210
to your ad behavior.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:14.170
Think about the questions that
your business is asking you

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:17.110
that you could answer if
you just had the CRM data

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.800
next to the ads impressions.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.440
How much more can you know and
empathize with your customer

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.570
if you just have
more data connected?

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.850
We're now at a point where
CRM and offline data,

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:31.770
like point of sale,
are in places where

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.810
that can be leveraged faster.

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.790
Together with ads
and marketing data,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:38.730
they can really help tell
both sides of the business

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.100
more things and help
them know and empathize

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:43.460
with your customers better.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.000
The mark tech and
the ad tech side

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.680
obviously we'll get
a lot from this,

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.740
but in time the production and
supply chain side will too.

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.300
So Cloud and Google
Marketing Platform together

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.500
make it possible
to build better,

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:02.150
more impactful campaigns and
add vital data to your CRM.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:04.790
This enables a better
personalization strategy,

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.060
keeping users engaged
and connected.

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.550
And that ensures that your
data is in a private and secure

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:12.740
place and is more thorough.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.080
And of course, I
think it's great.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:16.760
I work at Google.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.400
But Sharon, from
your perspective,

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.060
why should marketers care
about Google Marketing Platform

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:24.575
and Google Cloud together?

00:10:24.575 --> 00:10:26.450
SHARON HARRIS: Well,
Matt, it's super simple.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:28.367
And it's simple for
everyone in this audience.

00:10:28.367 --> 00:10:29.740
And we started with the why.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:33.950
40% of all businesses
will die in 10 years.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.572
The stakes are high.

00:10:35.572 --> 00:10:37.280
So when you think
about the Google Cloud,

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:41.180
think about an open
and secure environment.

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:45.350
When it comes to privacy
and safety of consumer data,

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:47.460
you can't afford anything less.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.970
And if you want to put your
business at risk because you've

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:55.100
chosen a platform that isn't
open, secure, and safe, good

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:56.540
luck.

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:57.690
Know the customer.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:58.630
We took the oath.

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:03.280
Know customer, be the customer,
empathize with the customer.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:05.890
Collecting and storing
data in the cloud

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.760
allows for faster analysis,
which really drives

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.530
your decision-making process.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:15.350
Remember, you have hard dollars,
real investments on the line.

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.550
If you're not making the
right decisions and choices,

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:21.820
they can have catastrophic
consequences for your business

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:23.210
today.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:27.710
Every second is precious
in this marketplace.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.020
Third, machine learning.

00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:31.820
Machine learning
gives you the ability

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.300
to solve very complex problems.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:39.560
And ML is already a component
of the Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:42.230
So you can easily leverage
it to advance your marketing

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:43.470
capabilities.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.470
And as a marketer
today, you really

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.570
want to be truly data-driven.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:48.650
So leverage ML.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.310
MATT MAGER: So what
does that look like?

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:55.352
For those of you who like
to look at architecture

00:11:55.352 --> 00:11:58.380
and are pulling their phones
up now to take pictures of it,

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.680
you've probably seen
slides like this already.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.440
We can bring in data from
the Google Marketing Platform

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.470
technologies, like Google
Analytics 360, Display &amp; Video

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:12.010
360, and Google Ads like
YouTube and AdWords data.

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:14.820
This can all be stored
in BigQuery storage.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:18.540
And with BigQuery Compute, you
can use Cloud Datalab and Cloud

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:24.720
ML to run SQL queries and
also to create clusters

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.760
for advanced segmentation.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.230
In terms of
visualization, I think

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.050
it's really important
to note that all of us

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:35.370
don't look at tabular
data all the time.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.017
It's really important
that, for those

00:12:37.017 --> 00:12:39.350
who are making decisions who
aren't going to be actually

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.990
building out the data
sets, that they understand

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:43.650
what's actually happening.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.040
So building out a visual
layer is really important.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.207
SHARON HARRIS: Make it easy.

00:12:47.207 --> 00:12:48.920
Make it easy for the marketer.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.910
I already have a hard job.

00:12:50.910 --> 00:12:54.350
I got 18 to 24 months
to show some results.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:56.660
Help me make the
data work for me.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.430
That's really the
key to visualization.

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.140
So I'm happy you're
doing that for me.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:00.890
MATT MAGER: Right.

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:02.420
And Data Studio is awesome.

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.000
But some people like to
use Looker or Tableau.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.410
Great tools.

00:13:06.410 --> 00:13:09.260
And so the Google
Cloud Platform allows

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:12.950
you to create visuals using
any tool that you'd like.

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:15.680
For those of us on the Google
Marketing Platform side,

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:17.930
once you've built
out the clusters

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:19.430
that you're interested
in using, you

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:22.580
can push those in through what's
called data import into Google

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:26.540
Analytics 360 to run ads
on Display &amp; Video 360

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.330
or across the Google Ads
products, like YouTube

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:30.470
or Search.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:33.440
It's a really great way
to very easily leverage

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:35.000
what you've already
got and do things

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:36.770
that you can't do
inside of the user

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:38.120
interfaces of those products.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.890
So on a high level,
a really easy example

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:50.020
of what we're doing
together with Deloitte today

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:52.290
looks like this.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:53.900
We start with BigQuery.

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:57.720
We use the most
awesome, easy to use,

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:01.250
simple tons of
connections, BigQuery

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.410
to take all of the data
sources that we have

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.550
and put them together.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:09.510
We add in the Display &amp; Video
360 data using Data Transfer

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:14.280
files, as well as Google Ads
data from YouTube and AdWords.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.010
And also, there's a
native BigQuery export

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:19.440
from Google Analytics 360
that allows you to put that

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.368
into BigQuery as well.

00:14:21.368 --> 00:14:23.910
Now for the client that we have
that we're already doing this

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.180
with, they've got some data from
point of sales, transaction,

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:29.640
and customer databases
that they wanted to add in,

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.420
so we use DataPrep to
structure that in a way that

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.070
can sit alongside the ads data.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.350
And then here's where
the magic happens.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.058
Yes, you're pulling
up your phones again.

00:14:42.058 --> 00:14:43.800
That's great.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.540
We get two things out of this.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.090
Once that data is
structured and put in a way

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:51.900
that we can use it, you
can get more analysis

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:53.080
and activation out of it.

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:55.560
And now for analysis,
it's important to note

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.390
that you can pass
the audiences back

00:14:57.390 --> 00:14:59.640
into the tools for
measurement and visualization.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:03.890
You can also just pull that into
a visualization tool itself.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:07.060
And then for activation,
within the Google Ads products,

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:10.720
like DV360 and ads for
YouTube and Search,

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.240
it's possible to actually
activate across segments

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:16.660
that you've built that have
both the Display Video 360

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:20.440
data, the Google Ads data,
and also the behavioral data

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:21.910
from your site.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.100
Again, this is a
very basic example,

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:26.540
even without getting into
machine learning or the AI

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:27.547
part.

00:15:27.547 --> 00:15:29.380
SHARON HARRIS: Now show
them the real magic.

00:15:31.972 --> 00:15:33.680
MATT MAGER: So this
is what people really

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:37.430
think that they need when
they first start, right?

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:38.780
It needs to be super complex.

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:40.245
It needs to solve
all my problems.

00:15:40.245 --> 00:15:41.870
It needs to solve
all of my challenges.

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:44.300
And every single
stakeholder, from the CEO

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.280
down to every
single coder, needs

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:49.318
to be in agreement
before we can do this.

00:15:49.318 --> 00:15:51.110
This is something that
Deloitte has already

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:52.745
suggested to some clients.

00:15:52.745 --> 00:15:54.870
And I think that we're
going to be building it out,

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.470
but it takes a long time to
get the stakeholders wrangled

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.440
together.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.320
Empathy is really complicated
is what this says.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:03.770
SHARON HARRIS: Very complicated.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:09.180
So remember that, empathy
is very, very complicated.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:12.130
MATT MAGER: But you really
can't start with this as a goal.

00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.950
This is too big to sell into
all the stakeholders necessary.

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.530
So we've always
suggested that you

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:22.450
start with something simple,
high value, and scalable.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:24.370
That way teams can
buy in on making

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:29.272
the project and the scope bigger
and more complex over time.

00:16:29.272 --> 00:16:30.730
If you start with
that first slide,

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.890
you're going to scare
off a lot of people.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.260
And we don't have time
to scare people off.

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.520
We need to bring them in.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.620
SHARON HARRIS: And as
the manager and marketer,

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:43.290
you must prove value
with clear goals.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:44.710
And you have to have quick wins.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:47.450
Once again, you're really
up against the clock.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:50.020
So you want to make sure
that what you're delivering

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:51.620
is going to really work.

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:53.710
So the construct
of test and learn

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:57.040
is the key to help
you close those gaps.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:00.600
Now, we're going to take a look
at some examples of very, very

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:04.192
straightforward retail
client use cases.

00:17:04.192 --> 00:17:06.609
And as the marker, there's
some frequent business problems

00:17:06.609 --> 00:17:08.240
that you're trying to solve.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:10.420
And we're always focused,
once again, what?

00:17:10.420 --> 00:17:12.880
Know the customer,
be the customer,

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:14.770
empathize with the customer.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:18.220
So these five are the
highest historical spenders

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:19.780
in your business.

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:23.760
Those online only shoppers,
you gotta love those folks.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.910
Heavy browsers
that don't log in.

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.580
So you're not getting
any authenticated data.

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:30.690
Heavy couponers.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.060
How many people love coupons?

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:33.730
Show of hands.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:34.230
That's it?

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:35.820
Nobody likes to save money?

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:39.510
Come on, get on the
coupon bandwagon.

00:17:39.510 --> 00:17:43.570
My favorite is loyal customers
that buy in-store and online.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:45.570
I have to raise my hand
really high for that one

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:48.510
because that is me
all day every day.

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:49.050
You too?

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:49.870
I knew it.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:50.430
I knew it.

00:17:50.430 --> 00:17:52.650
So as we think about
these use cases,

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:56.530
these are really core
to the retail space.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:18:00.720
And as we go through this,
we're to really focus on two.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:05.770
And the first, which is really
a foundational use case.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.250
This is all about,
how do you unlock

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:11.640
the data that you have so that
you can start to control it.

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.140
You can start to leverage it.

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:15.960
You can start to really
use it in your business.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.240
This is a critical
step in the journey.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:20.910
You really can't go
forward unless you

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:23.430
have this foundation
built. And this

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:28.080
is all about building an
advanced data infrastructure

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.000
for media insights.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.700
As the marketer,
you're always asked,

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:36.150
am I getting the value out of
my media spin that I should?

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:38.220
Am I reaching the
right audience?

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:40.440
Am I reaching them at
the right frequency?

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:41.940
Is it with the right message?

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:43.040
Who knows.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:45.850
And sometimes you really
can't ask your agency.

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:48.270
So what can you do
to really understand

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:49.680
the performance of it?

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.850
So Matt, tell us what do we do.

00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:54.570
MATT MAGER: Well, I can
tell you what you've done.

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:57.810
Deloitte has done this
for marketing already

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:01.470
using Data Transfer files
from Display &amp; Video 360.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:04.290
They're using BigQuery, and
R, and data visualizations,

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.710
and business intelligence
tools to derive detailed media

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:08.350
analytics.

00:19:08.350 --> 00:19:11.550
So what they've done is they've
taken those Data Transfer files

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:14.370
and they've done with them
more granular insights than you

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:18.410
can get from the user
interfaces of those tools alone.

00:19:18.410 --> 00:19:21.200
And then looking at
that granular data,

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:23.720
they have the ability
to more accurately look

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:26.420
at media metrics, including
reach and frequency.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.713
This seems like a base
case because it is.

00:19:28.713 --> 00:19:30.380
Again, we wanted to
start with something

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:34.100
simple, scalable, and of
high value because this

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:37.460
provides the infrastructure
to deliver advanced media

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:38.780
insights.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:40.670
So again, we're
suggesting for you

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:43.940
to start with something
simple and foundational.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.680
SHARON HARRIS: Prove your case.

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:47.330
Bring people over.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:48.655
That's really critical.

00:19:48.655 --> 00:19:50.030
MATT MAGER: And
so the value that

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.950
is derived from
doing this, as you're

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:55.560
traveling from the left side to
the right side, data comes in

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.900
and it's unstructured.

00:19:56.900 --> 00:19:58.610
Or it's structured,
but not in a way

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:01.350
that's congruous with the rest
of the data that you have.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:05.090
And as we provide more and more
structure and more and more

00:20:05.090 --> 00:20:08.930
details emerge, we can start
to get more and more insights.

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:12.190
So things like understanding
cross campaign conversion

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:15.140
and user overlap
is kind of basic.

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:18.217
But as you get a quick win, you
get more teams bought into it.

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:20.300
And then you can start
doing more advanced things,

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:23.690
like trending, analytics,
and visualizations

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:25.640
off of what you've
already built.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:28.910
And then starting to get into
predictive analytics, customer

00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:32.390
lifetime value, churn
propensity, et cetera.

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:35.960
And then you get to start
doing activation at scale.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:42.710
The true goal here is to be able
to do things quickly, scalably,

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:45.980
and automated, if you can.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.840
Again, this is the base
case for really anything,

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:50.970
including our second use case.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:55.212
SHARON HARRIS: Which is all
about data-driven segmentation.

00:20:55.212 --> 00:20:57.170
Now when you think of
data-driven segmentation,

00:20:57.170 --> 00:20:59.870
as the marketer, yes, you
want to know your customer.

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.480
But you need to know, is
this a loyal customer?

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:04.010
Is this a new customer?

00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:07.640
You want understand, can
I discover new customers?

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.820
Because that's ultimately
the real prize.

00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:12.770
And when we segment and
categorize customers,

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:15.200
that really drives our
decision-making process.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:18.530
And it ultimately leads to
greater efficiencies, which

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:20.810
give you stronger returns.

00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:24.550
So Matt, once again, how are
we going to pull this off?

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:26.600
MATT MAGER: So if you
remember that old slide,

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:29.870
collect, cluster,
visualize, and activate.

00:21:29.870 --> 00:21:31.280
It's almost the same story.

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:33.110
But what we can
do is we can take

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:36.920
that point of sale data, along
with site analytics and CRM

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.590
data, and run machine
learning clusters on it

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:42.290
to find meaningful
behavioral insights.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.290
So identifying user
attributes of each cluster

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:48.290
that, when users show up
and behave in certain ways,

00:21:48.290 --> 00:21:49.790
that we can then
put them quickly

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:51.530
into a segment that
might be able to be

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.870
activated or at least
be visualized so we

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:56.270
can start looking at trends.

00:21:56.270 --> 00:21:58.160
And then finally,
activating those clusters

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:01.850
when possible for media
buying on Display &amp; Video 360,

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:03.500
Google Ads, or other
media channels.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:08.570
So let's look at an example
of how we might apply that

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:10.430
to an actual user situation.

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:13.910
And because it was
just her birthday,

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:17.458
we've named my hypothetical
user on my mom, Audrey.

00:22:17.458 --> 00:22:20.000
SHARON HARRIS: Unfortunately we
couldn't bring Audrey with us

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.490
today, but she is here
with us in spirit,

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:24.687
cheering her beautiful
son, Matt, on.

00:22:24.687 --> 00:22:27.270
MATT MAGER: It would be a much
longer session if she was here.

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:28.130
Anyway.

00:22:28.130 --> 00:22:30.110
So we're going to say
that Audrey just went

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.020
online, and went to an
electronics retailer,

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:35.420
and bought a camera.

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:39.140
Now she might shop at that
electronics retailer online

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:42.260
pretty often, so we might
have some CRM data about her,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:46.160
and we have GA360 purchase data,
and maybe some behavioral data.

00:22:46.160 --> 00:22:48.140
So we have some
insights already.

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:52.250
Now with Display &amp; Video 360, or
with other activation channels,

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:56.720
we could do things like sending
her ads for the accessories

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:58.220
that she might need
for that camera,

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:02.780
or suggesting that she
bundle together memory cards.

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.710
But that's kind
of all we can do.

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:06.710
Let's say, things related
to exactly what you're

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:09.090
doing right now.

00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:11.850
But if we're adding things
like machine learning tools

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:15.540
across our entire CRM and across
all of the behavioral insights

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:18.900
that we can pull, we can
cluster Audrey, or my mom,

00:23:18.900 --> 00:23:22.320
into a group of people who
are frequent travelers.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.270
So we start getting
insights about people

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:26.760
who have bought cameras,
but have also bought

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:29.760
things like
international chargers

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:36.640
or lots of different cords for
things because they lose them.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.010
And we know that people
who are frequent travelers

00:23:39.010 --> 00:23:40.180
don't just buy cameras.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:42.130
They also buy noise
canceling headphones.

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:44.870
Who has noise canceling
headphones who travels a lot?

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:46.660
Yeah, exactly.

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:48.958
That kid in the fourth row.

00:23:48.958 --> 00:23:50.250
You know who I'm talking about.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:53.830
Anyway, so the reason
that that's important

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:56.140
is because then we can
start segmenting out

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:57.850
users who have done
some of the things

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:00.050
that we're interested
in, but not all of them.

00:24:00.050 --> 00:24:01.930
So we take this user, Audrey.

00:24:01.930 --> 00:24:04.420
We start to cluster
users like her

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:07.530
that have done things
like bought a camera,

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:09.550
are identified as a
frequent traveler,

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:11.520
but haven't bought
those headphones.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.410
And then we can tailor
ads to those people

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:17.760
specifically, like showing
them ads specific to,

00:24:17.760 --> 00:24:19.560
you're a frequent
traveler, Audrey.

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.230
We know you bought this camera
and here's some headphones

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.520
you might want, too.

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:25.890
Audrey sees an ad.

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:28.610
She has a higher likelihood
of buying those headphones

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:30.440
before her next trip.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.780
SHARON HARRIS: Or if
you're a really good son,

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:34.880
Mother's Day is approaching.

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.800
Sounds like a good gift for mom.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.430
MATT MAGER: I want her
